# Deploy Obot Sentry to macOS with Microsoft Intune

This download contains three files, all deployed to the same device group:

- `obot-sentry.pkg` — the obot-sentry device agent (version
  {{.obotSentryVersion}}), signed and notarized. Installs the agent and its
  two launchd jobs.
- `ai.obot.obot-sentry.mobileconfig` — the deployment configuration. The
  server URL and scan interval are already filled in; you supply the
  enrollment key.
- `ai.obot.obot-sentry.pppc.mobileconfig` — the file access the agent's
  background scans need. Upload as-is.

The file names are stable across versions, so upgrades never require editing
anything.

## 1. Add the installer

Obot Sentry installs a command-line tool rather than an `.app` bundle, and its
installer runs a script — packages that install outside `/Applications` and
carry scripts are exactly what Microsoft documents the **macOS app (PKG)** type
for. It requires the Intune management agent for macOS (version 2308.006 or
later), which Intune installs on the device the first time you assign a PKG app
or shell script to it.

1. In the Intune admin center, go to *Apps > macOS > Add*, select **macOS app
   (PKG)**, and upload `obot-sentry.pkg`.

2. **Detection rules:** set **Ignore app version** to **Yes**, then remove every
   entry from **Included apps**. That list is only for `.app` bundles the
   package installs, and Obot Sentry installs none — Microsoft documents that
   non-application entries left in it stop the app from ever reporting a
   successful install.

3. **Assign** the app as **Required** to the device groups that should report to
   this configuration.

To confirm an install on a device directly: `pkgutil --pkg-info ai.obot.obot-sentry`.

## 2. Push the configuration

1. Open `ai.obot.obot-sentry.mobileconfig` in a text editor and replace
   `REPLACE_WITH_ENROLLMENT_KEY` with your enrollment key.

2. Go to *Devices > Configuration > Create > New policy*, choose platform
   **macOS** and profile type **Templates > Custom**, then upload the edited
   file.

3. Assign it to the same device group.

To rotate the key or retune the scan interval later, edit the file, bump
`PayloadVersion` on both the inner and outer payload, and re-upload. Devices
pick the change up on their next profile sync — no reinstall.

## 3. Grant file access

Create a second **Templates > Custom** profile, upload
`ai.obot.obot-sentry.pppc.mobileconfig` as-is, and assign it to the same group.

Scans run in the background, where macOS cannot prompt for consent, so without
this profile the agent silently skips protected locations such as `~/Desktop`,
`~/Documents`, and `~/Downloads`. The profile pins Obot's Developer ID
signature, so it grants access to nothing else. macOS only honors these grants
on devices enrolled through Automated Device Enrollment or a user-approved
enrollment.

## What the installer does

- Installs `obot-sentry` to `/usr/local/bin`.
- Installs a LaunchAgent (`ai.obot.obot-sentry.scan`) that macOS runs in each
  signed-in user's session, as that user, at login and every 15 minutes. Each
  run submits a scan at most every {{.scanIntervalMinutes}} minutes
  (`ScanIntervalMinutes` in the configuration profile — change it there to
  retune without reinstalling).
- Installs a LaunchDaemon (`ai.obot.obot-sentry.hook-install`) that runs as
  root at load and hourly. It converges the managed local-agent audit hooks for
  the signed-in user, so hooks are re-checked for drift every hour.
- Prepares `/Library/Application Support/obot/obot-sentry`, where the device
  identity shared by every user on the Mac is stored.

Upgrades re-upload the same file name and reload both launchd jobs, carrying the
configuration forward: the profiles are untouched by an app update, and the
device identity and scan state live outside the package.

## Uninstall

Remove the hooks first: they invoke `obot-sentry` by path, so removing the agent
while its hooks are still configured leaves each coding agent trying to run a
command that no longer exists.

### 1. Remove the hooks

`hook-install` has no uninstall command, so removing the app stops future
convergence but leaves the hooks already written into each user's agent
configuration. Removing them is a per-user, per-device edit that Intune cannot
make on its own; run it as a shell script, or have each user run it.

Every entry Obot Sentry writes is marked with `--managed-by obot-sentry`. Find
the files that still carry one:

```sh
grep -rl 'managed-by obot-sentry' \
  "$HOME/.claude" "$HOME/.copilot" \
  "$HOME/Library/Application Support/Code/User" \
  /etc/codex "/Library/Application Support/Cursor" 2>/dev/null
```

- `$HOME/.copilot/hooks/obot-sentry.json` is written only by Obot Sentry —
  delete the whole file.
- `~/.claude/settings.json`, `/etc/codex/requirements.toml`, and
  `/Library/Application Support/Cursor/hooks.json` are shared with your own
  configuration — delete only the hook entries whose command contains
  `--managed-by obot-sentry` (the last two are machine-wide and need `sudo`).
- In `~/Library/Application Support/Code/User/settings.json`, remove the keys
  Obot Sentry added under `chat.hookFilesLocations` (`~/.copilot/hooks`,
  `.claude/settings.json`, `.claude/settings.local.json`,
  `~/.claude/settings.json`).

The per-user files exist once per signed-in user, so repeat for each user on a
shared Mac. Restart Claude Code, Codex, VS Code, and Cursor afterward to drop
the hooks.

### 2. Remove the agent

Unassign the app and both configuration profiles, then run `uninstall.sh` on
each device as a **shell script** (*Devices > Scripts > Add > macOS*), leaving
**Run script as signed-in user** set to **No**. It unloads both launchd jobs and
removes the binary, the plists, the package receipt, the shared device identity,
and every user's scan state. It is idempotent, so a device that already ran it
is unaffected.

A script is the only route: Intune's PKG app type has no **Uninstall**
assignment, and an app the Intune agent installed stays on the device when that
device is retired. Run this before retiring a Mac, while it is still managed.

To run it by hand on one device instead:

```sh
sudo bash uninstall.sh
```
